  "prompt_text": "Continue the text below with exactly one short, natural, coherent English sentence. Return only the continuation, with no preamble or explanation:\nWhen you're 28, science discovers a drug that stops all effects of aging, creating immortality. Your government decides to give the drug to all citizens under 26, but you and the rest of the “Lost Generations\" are deemed too high-risk. When you're 85, the side effects are finally discovered.\n\nI thought that there was a lot of hubub on the news, but I didn't know what it was all about until I sat down to really pay attention. \"It's a tragedy!\" the newscaster was gasping. \"The government has been hiding the side effects of the immortality drug for decades!\" I settled into my seat, feeling my knees creak and hearing my joints audibly pop. I winced. I'd always half felt sorry for myself that I was too old for the drug, but the other half of me knew, just knew that the other shoe would drop some day. I sighed and adjusted my pillow behind my aching back as I turned up the volume to listen. My ear weren't what they used to be.\n\n\"They're pulling the drug immediately!\" the newscaster was almost shrieking, and I could see her panic. \"They say the effects of the drug will wear off exactly 58 years after they were first administered!\" I could see the shock and disbelief of her terrifically youthful face. \"We're all going to get...old!\" She could barely get the word out. Like it was a curse. I felt a twinge of pity for her. Here I'd had my whole like to settle into myself and get used to the aches and pains, to get comfortable with the notion of my own mortality. These younger people who had lived their whole lives feeling immortal They were about to hit a hard reality.\n\nI turned off the TV and settled back into my chair. This would change everything, I realized. There would be repercussions all through society. People who had put off having kids because they had all the time in the world suddenly had no time. There weren't many kids around, I realized. The world could be in for a whole lot of trouble if its whole work force suddenly aged out of their ablilty to work. My dawning sense of dread was only slightly assuaged by the fact that I was too old to really feel the repercussions of this. I'd be dead before it really got bad. I pressed the button on my phone to call the assistant in my home. I loved it here, I mused, as I waited for her to respond. This retirement community was the perfect place to hide out and live my last decade or two in peace. I pushed the button again. And again. Finally I called the front desk.",
